<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS bundle, applied when Tauri signs it. Referenced by
  `bundle.macOS.entitlements` in `tauri.conf.json`.

  ## Why this file exists at all

  Under the hardened runtime — which notarisation requires, and which a
  Developer ID build gets — a process cannot open the microphone unless it says
  so here, whatever `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` in `Info.plist` says. The two
  are not alternatives: the plist key is what the *user* is asked, and this
  entitlement is what the *kernel* permits. The failure mode when only the plist
  is present is the quiet one this project has already been bitten by in another
  form: everything works in `tauri dev` and in an unsigned build, then capture
  returns silence in the signed, notarised bundle.

  ## What is deliberately absent

  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is **not** here. Sandboxing the app is a
  separate decision that has not been taken, and taking it silently as part of a
  microphone change would be the wrong way round: HANDOVER.md records that the
  App Store path is untested and that the sandbox is expected to break the
  `say`-based macOS pronunciation backend, which is a real problem to solve
  rather than a flag to flip. When that work happens, this file is where the
  sandbox entitlement belongs, and `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` below
  is already the one capture will need once it is sandboxed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
